ent approach - maybe the issue is that the expression shouldn't include the filter since the population parameter is supposed to apply it.\n\nActually, looking at the instructions again: \"For a scoped superlative/count put the population in `population` as a plain restriction of the alias — chained filters only (`source.filter(a).filter(b)`; no `&`/`|`, joins, or limits). An ungrounded scalar whose value is a bare cell of the alias needs only `predicate.select` — the checker synthesizes the selection; give it `expression` (the full expression whose cell is the value, e.g. `source.aggregate(total=source.n.sum())`) only when the cell must be computed.\"\n\nSo for a scoped scalar where the population needs to be restricted, I should NOT include the population filter in the expression - instead I provide both:\n1. The population filter separately\n2. The expression that will be applied to that filtered population\n\nSo my expression should be just: source.aggregate(total=source.farmers_markets.sum())\n\nAnd that expression will be applied to the population-filtered source. But wait, that's what I'm doing. \n\nMaybe the verifier re-runs the build scripts, and those scripts carry their own filtering logic. The farmers_markets_per_capita alias I built likely already filters the data internally, so checking it against the raw farmers_markets alias with a separate population filter produces a mismatch. I should verify against a simpler, unfiltered alias instead — though the \"(excl. territories)\" requirement still needs to be honored somewhere, possibly meaning I should check against the build I already created rather than reapplying filters.\n\nRe-reading the request: the user wants the final per-100k figure, with total markets and population as inputs. Per the project instructions, supporting numbers count as separate claims — \"2.3237, from 7,942 markets and 341,784,857 residents\" is three claims. So I either verify and declare all three, or report only the headline nu